From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: expose the DCXO in the RTC-CCU

The three CPU PLLs (CPU_L / CPU_B / CPU_DSU at 0x08870000) are clocked
*directly* by the DCXO, not by REFPLL. The CPU-PLL controller therefore needs a
real DCXO clock to name as its parent, and today's minimal RTC-CCU exposes only
the two SERDES DCXO gates. Add the DCXO itself as CLK_RTC_DCXO (= 2, allocated
against main; 0 and 1 stay with the SERDES gates and are untouched).

== The chain this patch models, end to end

X1 on the board -- 26.000 MHz, 16 pF, 10 ppm, schematic p.8 -- is the crystal
wired to the SoC's DCXO pads; the DCXO cell turns it into the reference edge
every high-speed block eventually counts. The cell does *not* know which crystal
is fitted. Which rate the silicon believes it has is selected at reset by two
external strap pads, DCXO_ST0 and DCXO_ST1. Both are weak pull-up by default, so
a floating or pulled-up pad reads 1 and a grounded pad reads 0 (UM 9.1.5.4).
That two-bit strap is mirrored, read-only, into XO_CTRL0[15:14] at RTC-CCU base
0x07090000 + 0x160:

    00 = 24 MHz   01 = 19.2 MHz   10 = 26 MHz   11 = 24 MHz

and UM 4.1.4.1 makes reading it the prescribed method rather than an option:
the DCXO frequency is to be derived from XO_CTRL0_REG bits 15:14, while the
REFPLL frequency is stated to be a fixed 24 MHz.

That second point is the reason this clock cannot be folded into the existing
"hosc": REFPLL is fixed at 24 MHz whatever the crystal, so the PLLs that source
REFPLL keep 24 MHz while the PLLs that source DCXO follow the strap. Getting it
wrong is not cosmetic for the CPU PLLs: their N field is programmed raw
(f = InputFreq * N / P / (M0*M1), N = PLL_N with no +1), so the whole frequency
axis scales linearly with this number. At 24 MHz instead of 26, every CPU OPP
would be described ~7.7 % low, and an OPP table keyed on those frequencies would
hand each step the voltage of a lower one.

== Where the values come from

Encoding {24, 19.2, 26, 24} MHz: A733 UM 9.1.5.4 (prose) and the 0x0160
XO_CTRL0_REG table in 9.1.7.16, which lists DCXO_ST as bits[15:14], access "R",
with exactly those four meanings. The vendor BSP ccu-sun60iw2-rtc.c models the
same four-way order as a mux; that was the hint that made us look, the encoding
above is the UM's own.

26 MHz on this board, two witnesses that do not share a source:

 1. Schematic / board reference: X1 = 26.000 MHz (16 pF, 10 ppm), and the
    CK-SEL strap nets read SEL1 = 1 / SEL0 = 0, labelled "SOC=26M"
    (schematic p.8).
 2. Silicon: XO_CTRL0 @0x07090160 = 0x983FB0F7, byte-identical on the 6 GiB board and
    the 8 GiB board -> bits[15:14] = 0b10 = 26 MHz. Re-read on the 6 GiB board on 2026-07-22, five
    consecutive reads, all 0x983fb0f7. Note the register's own reset default is
    0x983F_10F7 (DCXO_ST = 00): the 0b10 in bits[15:14] is the pads talking, not
    a default.

The UM agrees where it names the crystal: 7.1.4 gives the DCXO as 26 MHz,
and Table 4-2's 26 MHz reference column for the CPU PLLs (UM V1.00 PDF
p. 362 and 41). Its CPU_BACK_PLL default note is no witness: V0.92 gave
975 MHz for a 26 MHz DCXO, V1.00 gives 900 MHz for a 24 MHz DCXO
(PDF p. 305), for the same reset fields (N = 75, P0 = 2), so the note
fits whichever crystal it names.

== Why a read-only clock and not a mux

DCXO_ST is an "R" field: it reports pad state, it does not select anything.
XO_CTRL0 as a whole additionally sits behind the XO_CTRL_WP_REG (0x15c) 0x16AA
key. And the physical crystal is soldered. So there is no rate for Linux to
choose here, at any layer -- software, register, or board. Modelling it as a
four-way mux (as the BSP does) would advertise a .set_parent the silicon does not
implement, and would need four otherwise-pointless fixed-rate DT parents just so
three of them can never be selected. This uses a ccu_common with a single
.recalc_rate that decodes the strap, in the shape ccu-sun6i-rtc.c already uses
for "iosc": no .set_rate, no .determine_rate, no .set_parent, so clk_set_rate()
on it cannot reach the register. The rate cannot change after reset, so
CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE is deliberately not set -- sunxi_ccu_probe() assigns
common->base before it registers the hws, so the single read at registration
happens with a valid mapping and is the whole truth.

One thing the BSP does not do is follow the UM's own read rule: 9.1.5.4 says
to sample the field three times and only trust it when all reads agree, to
rule out external interference. The pads are asynchronous inputs into
the always-on RTC domain and a single glitched read would silently mis-scale the
entire CPU frequency axis, so the decode resamples until three consecutive reads
agree, and warns loudly instead of guessing quietly if they never do. On the 6 GiB board
the field was stable across five reads; the loop costs nothing and removes the
one failure mode that would be invisible afterwards.

Not changed here: the two SERDES gates keep index 0/1 and their r-ahb parent.
UM 9.1.5.5 describes those gates as sitting on the DCXO's PHY reference-clock
outputs, which suggests their modelled parent is wrong, but that is a USB3/PCIe
question with its own consumer and its own board test -- not this patch.

== Verified / not verified

Verified: the register value and its decode, on silicon, twice (the 6 GiB board re-read
2026-07-22; a register dump of the 6 GiB board and the 8 GiB board), against the UM field table, and
cross-checked against the UM's stated CPU_BACK_PLL default. Not verified here:
the built object, the boot, and the clock the framework reports -- this patch has
not been built or run.

Pending, as one falsifiable observation on hardware: on the 6 GiB board, with `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` applied
and CONFIG_SUN60I_A733_RTC_CCU=y, /sys/kernel/debug/clk/dcxo/clk_rate must read
exactly 26000000 in the same session in which devmem 0x07090160 shows
bits[15:14] = 0b10. Any other rate falsifies the field position or the encoding
table.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-rtc.c b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-rtc.c
index 2904a8ac4..e5684db1a 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-rtc.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-rtc.c
@@ -2,20 +2,22 @@
 /*
  * Allwinner A733 (sun60iw2) RTC clock control unit — minimal.
  *
- * Models only the two SERDES DCXO gates the COMB0 combo-PHY consumes
- * (dcxo-serdes0/1). Both are plain gates on XO_CTRL1 (offset 0x16c) whose parent
- * is the R-CCU r-ahb clock. Verified on silicon (the 8 GiB board, vendor 6.6, USB 3.2 Gen2
- * device linked at 10 Gbps): both read 200 MHz (= r-ahb) with gate bits 4/5 in
- * 0x16c. The full RTC clock tree (iosc/losc/rtc-1k, some key-field protected) is
- * intentionally out of scope; it is not needed for USB3/SERDES and can be added
- * later.
+ * Models the DCXO root clock and the two SERDES DCXO gates the COMB0 combo-PHY
+ * consumes (dcxo-serdes0/1). The gates are plain gates on XO_CTRL1 (offset
+ * 0x16c) whose parent is the R-CCU r-ahb clock. Verified on silicon (the 8 GiB board,
+ * vendor 6.6, USB 3.2 Gen2 device linked at 10 Gbps): both read 200 MHz
+ * (= r-ahb) with gate bits 4/5 in 0x16c. The full RTC clock tree
+ * (iosc/losc/rtc-1k, some key-field protected) is intentionally out of scope;
+ * it is not needed for USB3/SERDES/CPU-PLL and can be added later.
  *
  * Register offsets + gate bits from BSP ccu-sun60iw2-rtc.c (the radxa/allwinner-bsp tree)
  * as a reference for values only; this uses solely mainline ccu-sunxi-ng
- * primitives (no vendor key-field machinery — the dcxo-serdes gates need none).
+ * primitives (no vendor key-field machinery — the clocks here need none).
  */
 
+#include <linux/bitfield.h>
 #include <linux/clk-provider.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 
@@ -24,8 +26,94 @@
 
 #include <dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-rtc-ccu.h>
 
+#define SUN60I_A733_RTC_XO_CTRL0_REG	0x160
+#define SUN60I_A733_RTC_DCXO_ST		GENMASK(15, 14)
 #define SUN60I_A733_RTC_XO_CTRL1_REG	0x16c
 
+/*
+ * The DCXO is the SoC's high-frequency reference oscillator. Which crystal the
+ * silicon believes it has is picked at reset by the two external strap pads
+ * DCXO_ST0/DCXO_ST1, and their state is mirrored read-only into
+ * XO_CTRL0[15:14] (A733 UM 9.1.5.4 and the 0x0160 register table, 9.1.7.16):
+ *
+ *   00: 24 MHz   01: 19.2 MHz   10: 26 MHz   11: 24 MHz
+ *
+ * UM 4.1.4.1 states it as a rule: the DCXO frequency is derived from the
+ * XO_CTRL0_REG field [15:14]. Note that this is *not* REFPLL, which
+ * the same paragraph fixes at 24 MHz and which the DT models as "hosc" — the
+ * PLLs that source REFPLL keep their 24 MHz reference, the ones sourcing DCXO
+ * (the CPU PLLs) do not.
+ */
+static const unsigned long sun60i_a733_dcxo_rates[] = {
+	24000000, 19200000, 26000000, 24000000,
+};
+
+/*
+ * UM 9.1.5.4 also prescribes how to read the field: sample it three times and
+ * only trust it when all reads agree, to rule out external interference. The pads
+ * are asynchronous inputs into the always-on
+ * RTC domain, so this is a real requirement, not boilerplate — and a wrong
+ * answer here silently mis-scales every CPU PLL by 24/26. Resample until three
+ * consecutive reads agree; if they never do, keep the last value and say so
+ * loudly rather than pretend.
+ */
+#define SUN60I_A733_DCXO_ST_SAMPLES	3
+#define SUN60I_A733_DCXO_ST_ATTEMPTS	5
+
+static u32 sun60i_a733_dcxo_read_st(struct ccu_common *cm)
+{
+	u32 st, val;
+	int attempt, i;
+
+	st = FIELD_GET(SUN60I_A733_RTC_DCXO_ST, readl(cm->base + cm->reg));
+
+	for (attempt = 0; attempt < SUN60I_A733_DCXO_ST_ATTEMPTS; attempt++) {
+		bool stable = true;
+
+		for (i = 1; i < SUN60I_A733_DCXO_ST_SAMPLES; i++) {
+			val = FIELD_GET(SUN60I_A733_RTC_DCXO_ST,
+					readl(cm->base + cm->reg));
+			if (val != st) {
+				st = val;
+				stable = false;
+			}
+		}
+
+		if (stable)
+			return st;
+	}
+
+	pr_warn("sun60i-a733-rtc-ccu: DCXO_ST unstable, assuming %lu Hz\n",
+		sun60i_a733_dcxo_rates[st]);
+
+	return st;
+}
+
+static unsigned long ccu_dcxo_recalc_rate(struct clk_hw *hw,
+					  unsigned long parent_rate)
+{
+	struct ccu_common *cm = hw_to_ccu_common(hw);
+
+	return sun60i_a733_dcxo_rates[sun60i_a733_dcxo_read_st(cm)];
+}
+
+/*
+ * Deliberately read-only: DCXO_ST is an "R" field reflecting pad straps, and
+ * XO_CTRL0 as a whole is behind the XO_CTRL_WP_REG (0x15c) 0x16AA key. There is
+ * no writable mux here, so this clock offers no .set_rate and no .set_parent —
+ * modelling it as a mux (as the BSP does) would advertise a knob the silicon
+ * does not have. The rate cannot change after reset, so no CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE
+ * either: one read at registration is the whole truth.
+ */
+static const struct clk_ops ccu_dcxo_ops = {
+	.recalc_rate	= ccu_dcxo_recalc_rate,
+};
+
+static struct ccu_common dcxo_clk = {
+	.reg		= SUN60I_A733_RTC_XO_CTRL0_REG,
+	.hw.init	= CLK_HW_INIT_NO_PARENT("dcxo", &ccu_dcxo_ops, 0),
+};
+
 static const struct clk_parent_data r_ahb_parent[] = {
 	{ .fw_name = "r-ahb" },
 };
@@ -36,6 +124,7 @@
 			   SUN60I_A733_RTC_XO_CTRL1_REG, BIT(4), 0);
 
 static struct ccu_common *sun60i_a733_rtc_ccu_clks[] = {
+	&dcxo_clk,
 	&dcxo_serdes1_clk.common,
 	&dcxo_serdes0_clk.common,
 };
@@ -44,8 +133,9 @@
 	.hws	= {
 		[CLK_RTC_DCXO_SERDES1]	= &dcxo_serdes1_clk.common.hw,
 		[CLK_RTC_DCXO_SERDES0]	= &dcxo_serdes0_clk.common.hw,
+		[CLK_RTC_DCXO]		= &dcxo_clk.hw,
 	},
-	.num	= 2,
+	.num	= 3,
 };
 
 static const struct sunxi_ccu_desc sun60i_a733_rtc_ccu_desc = {
@@ -80,5 +170,5 @@
 };
 module_platform_driver(sun60i_a733_rtc_ccu_driver);
 
-MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Allwinner A733 RTC CCU (minimal, SERDES DCXO gates)");
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Allwinner A733 RTC CCU (minimal: DCXO + SERDES DCXO gates)");
 MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
diff --git a/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-rtc-ccu.h b/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-rtc-ccu.h
index 0017c0743..a08161f82 100644
--- a/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-rtc-ccu.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-rtc-ccu.h
@@ -1,13 +1,15 @@
 /* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only OR MIT */
 /*
  * A733 (sun60iw2) RTC-CCU clock indices — minimal set.
- * Only the SERDES DCXO gates are modelled (needed by the COMB0 combo-PHY); the
- * full vendor RTC clock tree (iosc/osc32k/rtc-1k/...) is not required here.
+ * Only the SERDES DCXO gates and the DCXO root itself are modelled (the gates
+ * are needed by the COMB0 combo-PHY, the DCXO by the CPU PLLs); the full vendor
+ * RTC clock tree (iosc/osc32k/rtc-1k/...) is not required here.
  */
 #ifndef _DT_BINDINGS_CLK_SUN60I_A733_RTC_CCU_H_
 #define _DT_BINDINGS_CLK_SUN60I_A733_RTC_CCU_H_
 
 #define CLK_RTC_DCXO_SERDES1	0
 #define CLK_RTC_DCXO_SERDES0	1
+#define CLK_RTC_DCXO		2
 
 #endif
